# Merge behaviour for the two files every branch touches.
#
# Measured in notes/branch-triage-2026-08-10.md: across the fifteen open
# branches, `uv.lock` and `notes/README.md` alone produced 16 of the 50 pairwise
# merge conflicts. Neither was ever a real disagreement — one is generated, the
# other is an append-only index — so both are resolved here rather than by hand,
# once per branch, forever.

# The lockfile is generated from pyproject.toml, so a line-by-line merge of two
# resolutions is meaningless at best and produces an internally inconsistent
# lockfile at worst: interleaved package stanzas that neither branch ever
# resolved and that no `uv sync` would ever produce.
#
# Resolution is split across two hooks because neither can do the job alone:
#   scripts/merge-uv-lock.sh   takes HEAD's lockfile verbatim, so the result is
#                              always a resolution some branch really produced.
#   .githooks/post-merge       re-resolves it against the merged pyproject.toml.
# The driver cannot do the second step itself — it runs before git writes the
# merged files and before MERGE_HEAD exists, so it cannot see the merged
# manifest, and resolving there yields a lockfile that is silently stale. That
# was measured, not assumed; the long comment in the driver has the details.
#
# Both halves are registered per clone (.claude/hooks/session-start.sh does it,
# and the README quickstart lists it). Without registration git falls back to the
# built-in 3-way merge, so this is a cleanup, never a correctness dependency.
uv.lock merge=uv-lock

# Generated, and large: keep it out of language stats and collapsed in diffs.
uv.lock linguist-generated=true

# Append-only index. Every branch adds its own entry at the end, and the entries
# are independent, so taking both sides is always the right answer — that is
# exactly what `union` does. The failure mode is a duplicated line when two
# branches document the same note, which is visible and trivially fixed; the
# alternative is a conflict on every single branch, which is what we measured.
#
# Deliberately scoped to this one index rather than `*.md`: prose files DO have
# real conflicts, and silently unioning two rewrites of the same paragraph would
# produce a document that reads as though it were merged cleanly when it was not.
notes/README.md merge=union
